Yes, absolutely. A common misconception is that a Work-For-Hire agreement means a one-time flat fee with no backend. Legally, the *ownership* of the copyright and the *compensation* structure are two separate things. 

You can execute a Producer Agreement that explicitly states the beat is a 'work-made-for-hire' (meaning you are the sole legal author and 100% owner of the master recording from inception), while simultaneously including a contractual obligation for you to pay them a 2% master royalty on the backend. In this scenario, the initial flat fee acts as an advance against those future royalties. <<<SYSTEM_ORCHESTRATION>>> {"target": "royalties", "action": "configure_producer_points"} 

Make sure the contract also addresses the publishing—beatmakers typically retain their writer's share (often 50% of the composition) unless you explicitly buy that out as well. ⚖️ I am an AI assistant, not a licensed attorney. Have an entertainment lawyer draft or review the Producer Agreement to ensure the copyright assignment is bulletproof.
